Stop a forked child from running copies of the parent's greenlets.

Only the greenlet that forked survives a fork, the same way only the calling
thread does. Everything else in the parent's hub is a copy, and a child that
resumes one redoes work the parent has already done or is doing.

``gevent.threading._ForkHooks`` already made those copies *appear* stopped to
:mod:`threading`, but anything on the loop's callback queue is a pending switch
into one of them, so the child's hub resumed them as soon as anything yielded.
The first thing that can yield is the next
``os.register_at_fork(after_in_child=)`` handler, which runs inside ``fork()``
before it has returned to its caller. Under monkey-patching such handlers do
yield: ``Thread.start()`` waits on an ``Event``, a patched lock may be held by
another greenlet, ``logging`` takes one. A child that did nothing at all but
``os._exit()`` was measured running four of the parent's greenlets first, on
every one of 20 forks.

Those queued switches are now cancelled in the child. Greenlets parked on a
timer or an ``io`` watcher are resumed by the loop rather than by that queue,
and this change does not address them; for the child of
:mod:`gevent.subprocess`, which may run nothing at all before it ``exec``s, see
the separate fix that gives it a hub of its own.
